  "notes": "Drawn from Feb 2026 photos of N8710M. The fuselage is a continuous sunrise gradient (periwinkle at the nose through magenta, coral and orange to yellow at the tail): drawn as one gradient. Re-checked 2026-11-15: the fin is the state jets' Heart fin as re-measured on Colorado One (the same fin on every 737), copied with its 'Southwest' title and flag. 'Imua One' measured on the 2023 right-side photo against the nose and the forward door (2.5-3.45 m back), two weights, a pair of titles per side (the left mirrored; the 2026 photos are from behind and too oblique to read it), on Colorado One's line under the cockpit windows. The sunrise bands are a gradient, kept as straight cuts. Drawn on the 737-8 (no measured shape; it's within 0.1 m of the 737-800's fuselage, so shapes/B738.json is close). Redrawn 2026-11-28: the black pattern is traced per side in short stretches along the body (left from the Feb 2026 photos, right from the Sept 2024 Santa Barbara photo; the two sides differ in detail). The sunrise is one gradient, yellow aft of x 0.72 (ahead of the fin root). The belly between the engines is not seen; the pattern stops above it."
}
